steps.text:
Yes — this is a bug in that early `@apply` implementation.

`border-dotted` is generated twice:

```css
.border-dotted { border-width: 0; }
...
.border-dotted { border-style: dotted; }
```

With classes, `.border` appears between those generated rules and supplies a nonzero width, so `class="border border-dotted"` works.

But `@apply border-dotted` copies both matching rules directly into your rule:

```css
.example {
  border-width: 0;
  border-style: dotted;
}
```

So there is no border to draw. You’re reading the linked lines correctly.

A practical workaround in that version is:

```css
.example {
  @apply border-dotted;
  border-width: 1px;
}
```

or simply:

```css
.example {
  border: 1px dotted #dae4e9;
}
```

The proper fix would be to avoid making the style utility depend on a separate same-selector reset rule (or make `@apply` understand that composition).
